            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Lee T. Paterson, the Ninth Circuit has a treating\u2011physician rule in ERISA cases that requires a plan administrator to either accept the treating physician\u2019s opinion or reject it with specific legitimate reasons based on substantial evidence."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "American Medical Association",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0002-0006"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 52,
              "start": 52
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to American Medical Association, the American Medical Association reports that 39\u202f% of treating physicians misrepresent symptoms, diagnosis, and severity of illness when patients submit insurance claims."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0003-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 53,
              "start": 53
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The petitioner argues that treating physicians\u2019 misrepresentations give them special weight, deference, and a presumption in ERISA disability\u2011benefit determinations."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 19,
              "start": 19
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The respondent notes that the Secretary\u2019s 2002 regulations imposed many new requirements on plan administrators, taking a different approach from earlier practice."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 19,
              "start": 19
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Blatt, the respondent contends there is no evidence that the plan considered the treating physician\u2019s opinion."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0001-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 23,
              "start": 23
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "If an explanation is inadequate, the proper consequence is a remand to the administrator for further explanation, not a de\u202fnovo standard of review."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Rohlfing",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 36,
              "start": 36
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Rohlfing, the respondent argues that the treating\u2011physician rule is unrelated to the concept of actual bias."
      }
